WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.340
Scope defines what the review is allowed
to examine and what it should avoid.

2
00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:13.618
A container security review may include
application repositories,

3
00:00:13.698 --> 00:00:21.393
container images, registries, Dockerfiles,
compose files, Kubernetes manifests,

4
00:00:21.473 --> 00:00:26.389
Helm charts, CI/CD pipelines, runtime
environments,

5
00:00:26.469 --> 00:00:30.423
and cloud or orchestration configuration.

6
00:00:30.503 --> 00:00:36.915
The scope should name the in-scope
repositories, images, namespaces,

7
00:00:36.995 --> 00:00:41.483
clusters, environments, and review
activities.

8
00:00:41.563 --> 00:00:46.479
Rules of engagement should also define
data handling.

9
00:00:46.559 --> 00:00:52.330
Container reviews can expose secrets,
environment variables,

10
00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:58.075
internal package names, source code,
vulnerability reports,

11
00:00:58.155 --> 00:01:06.170
registry metadata, deployment manifests,
and client-specific architecture details.

12
00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:13.837
AI-assisted workflows should keep that
material inside approved systems and avoid

13
00:01:13.917 --> 00:01:17.978
unnecessary disclosure to public services.

14
00:01:18.058 --> 00:01:24.043
Sensitive values should be minimized or
redacted where possible.

15
00:01:24.123 --> 00:01:29.146
Client boundaries and environment
boundaries matter.

16
00:01:29.226 --> 00:01:36.707
A development image may be in scope while
production runtime configuration is not.

17
00:01:36.787 --> 00:01:42.344
A registry may contain images from
multiple teams or tenants.

18
00:01:42.424 --> 00:01:48.729
A Kubernetes cluster may host workloads
unrelated to the engagement.

19
00:01:48.809 --> 00:01:56.718
AI-generated checklists and summaries
should be reviewed against those boundaries so

20
00:01:56.798 --> 00:02:00.538
the work remains authorized and focused.

21
00:02:00.618 --> 00:02:05.214
Avoid out-of-scope testing or intrusive
actions.

22
00:02:05.294 --> 00:02:11.385
A review can often be performed from
artifacts such as Dockerfiles,

23
00:02:11.465 --> 00:02:16.595
manifests, scanner reports, SBOMs,
registry metadata,

24
00:02:16.675 --> 00:02:20.095
and approved configuration exports.

25
00:02:20.175 --> 00:02:23.381
When runtime validation is needed,

26
00:02:23.461 --> 00:02:29.552
it should follow the agreed plan and
change-control expectations.

27
00:02:29.632 --> 00:02:36.686
Scope is what keeps AI assistance helpful
instead of turning it into a source of

28
00:02:36.766 --> 00:02:37.300
risk.
